WEBVTT

NOTE The Lost World (1925) - Riah TV captions

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.900
[music]

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:14.990
The Lost World

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.990
Presented by Earl Hudson.
Adapted by Marion Fairfax

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.990
from the novel by Sir
Arthur Conan Doyle.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.990
Directed by Harry O. Hoyt.
Photographed by Arthur Edeson.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:34.990
Art director: Milton Menasco.
Film editor: George McGuire.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.990
Technical director:
Willis H. O'Brien,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.990
with Marcel Delgado
and Ralph Hammeras.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.990
Fred W. Jackman and
the technical staff.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.070
Paula White: Bessie Love.
Sir John Roxton: Lewis Stone.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.160
Professor Challenger: Wallace
Beery. Edward Malone: Lloyd
Hughes.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.240
Gladys Hungerford: Alma Bennett.
Professor Summerlee: Arthur
Hoyt.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.320
Mrs. Challenger: Margaret
McWade. Ape-man: Bull Montana.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.410
Austin: Finch Smiles.
Zambo: Jules Cowles.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.500
Colin McArdle: George Bunny.
Major Hibbard: Charles
Wellesley.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.250
Introducing Sir Arthur Conan
Doyle, author of the story.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:37.250
"I have wrought my simple plan /
If I give one hour of joy / To
the boy who's half a man, / Or
the man who's half a boy."

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:33.250
"But Gladys, suppose I never
get a chance at the big things?"

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:54.750
"The kind of man I mean makes
his own chances. You can't hold
him back!"

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:21.750
In the office of the
London Record-Journal.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:42.500
"I want your legal advice.
Professor Challenger threatens
to sue my paper for doubting his
yarn about live dinosaurs."

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:25.250
"I believe Challenger is insane!
He nearly killed three reporters
I sent to interview him today!"

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:42.500
"Mr. McArdle, couldn't you send
me on a dangerous assignment?
All I need is an opportunity —"

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:07.250
"You seem very anxious
to lose your life!"

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:24.000
"Have you been interviewing
Challenger, Malone?"

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:55.250
"Don't apologize — hurry over to
Zoological Hall and cover
Challenger's lecture. Reporters
are barred — but get in!"

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:45.500
"There's Sir John Roxton —
famous hunter and explorer. He's
here to check up on Challenger's
cock-and-bull story."

00:06:55.500 --> 00:07:09.250
"Well, Malone, we're in for a
lively session tonight — the
students are out in force to
heckle old Challenger."

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:28.938
"Challenger has made himself a
public laughing stock by his
tale about live dinosaurs! What
can he hope to gain by such

00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:29.500
lies?"

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:11.750
"But how can Challenger's story
be true? These creatures have
all been dead ten million
years!"

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:24.250
"You might at least do him the
justice to hear him state his
own case."

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:44.000
"Reporters are barred — if I
show my press pass I'll get
kicked out. I wonder if you —"

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:52.750
"Gladly — I'll take
you in on my pass."

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:20.000
"That's Professor Summerlee,
the eminent coleopterist."

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:12.000
"Bring on your mastodons!
Bring on your mammoths!"

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:24.845
"I will — if any of you
spineless worms are brave enough
to go back with me into the
trackless jungles where these

00:10:24.855 --> 00:10:26.250
monsters live!"

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:35.500
"I told you old
Challenger is sincere!"

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:44.500
"So it's when I ask for
volunteers to look death or
something worse in the face that
you become silent!"

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:56.250
"I see I offended you by calling
you 'worms'. Well, I offer you
worms an apology and beg your
worms' forgiveness."

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:13.500
"Queer combination, old
Challenger. One of the greatest
minds in London, with the temper
— of a gorilla."

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:25.799
"And I'm not here tonight to
defend my statements — but to
demand that a committee be
formed to go back to the Lost

00:11:25.809 --> 00:11:27.000
World with me —"

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:40.750
"And if any of us should live to
tell the tale, then perhaps our
testimony will satisfy these
gentlemen!"

00:11:51.000 --> 00:12:03.750
"I am nearly sixty years of age
— but, because I think you are a
liar and a fraud — I accept your
challenge, sir!"

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:17.000
"Accepted! Better an old fool —"

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:26.500
"— than a young coward!"

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:40.000
"I'm neither a student nor a
scientist — but I'd like to be
counted in on this!"

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:52.264
"My friend, Sir John Roxton!
Accepted! Your record as a
hunter of big game will add
weight to your testimony — if we

00:12:52.274 --> 00:12:53.000
return!"

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:11.000
"Professor Challenger, I'd
like to go on this expedition!"

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:22.500
"Probably the brain of a child —
but the body of an athlete.
Accepted. What is your name?"

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:27.500
"Edward E. Malone —"

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:34.000
"And your occupation?"

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:44.750
"A newspaper reporter, sir.
London Record-Journal."

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:20.000
"Take me home — I've had enough
of this! Eleven Enmore Park,
Kensington, West!"

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:42.000
"Professor Challenger, I've got
to go on this expedition! The
girl I'm engaged to won't marry
me until I've faced death or —"

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:15.250
"This man attacked me!"

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:22.750
"Do you give him
in charge, sir?"

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:31.250
"No. I was to blame.
I intruded upon him."

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:55.500
"Now, sir, can't we talk this
expedition over sanely? Sir John
Roxton is a friend of mine —"

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:04.250
"Why didn't you
mention that before?"

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:34.500
"You've hurt another nice young
man! I saw you from my window!"

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:47.750
"My wife — Mr. Malone."

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:10.000
"If my husband gets abusive
again — just call me!"

00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:33.250
"Sit down!"

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:41.250
"Sit down!"

00:18:51.500 --> 00:19:04.000
"As I told you, I've personal
reasons for wanting to give up
my job on the paper and join
your expedition. I —"

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:23.500
"Austin!"

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:39.000
"Tell Miss White I wish
to see her at once!"

00:20:00.250 --> 00:20:03.000
"Have you shown
Malone — the diary?"

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:26.841
"Miss Paula White — daughter of
poor Maple White — the
unfortunate explorer whose
notebook you hold in your

00:21:26.851 --> 00:21:27.250
hands."

00:21:44.750 --> 00:21:52.250
"Miss White was her father's
trained assistant. She will tell
you why she came to me."

00:21:57.750 --> 00:22:06.000
"I was in camp with jungle fever
when my father went up to
explore that terrible plateau."

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:20.250
"He would go — in spite
of what we had seen."

00:22:26.750 --> 00:22:39.732
"You mean that you actually saw
living descendants of these
monsters that are supposed to
have been dead for millions of

00:22:39.742 --> 00:22:40.500
years?"

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:36.654
"My husband promised to raise
funds for a rescue party — but
instead of getting people to
help him, he throws them out of

00:23:36.664 --> 00:23:37.750
houses and things!"

00:23:49.250 --> 00:23:54.000
"Why, this is a great
human interest story —"

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:06.526
"And even if my paper was not
convinced of the scientific
value of your statements — it
might finance a rescue party —

00:24:06.536 --> 00:24:09.250
provided that the exclusive
publication rights —"

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:21.750
"I'll have nothing to do
with the Record-Journal!"

00:24:29.750 --> 00:24:37.750
"Surely you won't let your
personal feelings rob us of this
hope of rescuing my father!"

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:50.500
"Surely, George, a rescue party
is a great deal better than a
lawsuit!"

00:25:07.750 --> 00:25:17.250
"If Sir John Roxton will go with
me — I believe we can make my
managing editor see this thing!"

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:53.000
"I'm sure you know why I am
going on this rescue party,
Paula — don't you?"

00:29:50.750 --> 00:30:01.000
"We start in ten minutes! Anyone
who isn't ready... remains
behind."

00:30:11.750 --> 00:30:16.000
"If that mischievous beast is
to go with us, I remain behind!"

00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:30.272
"Jocko knows which berries and
roots in the forest are edible,
and which are poisonous — he
will be far more use to us than

00:30:30.282 --> 00:30:31.750
you, my dear sir!"

00:30:59.250 --> 00:31:00.250
[Newspaper] EXPLORERS REACH
LAST OUTPOST OF CIVILIZATION

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:24.250
"It's a Brazilian sloth — they
always walk head downward. See
the baby clinging to her?"

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:17.250
"They're not cubs — but full
grown 'spectacle bears' — great
camp followers."

00:36:21.500 --> 00:36:28.250
"In the morning we'll climb the
pinnacle — and drop the other
tree over."

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:43.250
"A Pterodactyl — proving
definitely that the statements
in poor Maple White's diary are
true!"

00:39:11.250 --> 00:39:17.500
"I told Austin to keep Jocko
down in the camp until we get
back tonight!"

00:43:29.750 --> 00:43:36.000
"A Brontosaurus — feeding merely
on leaves. Perfectly harmless —
unless it happens to step on
us."

00:43:49.500 --> 00:43:54.250
"My elephant gun might as
well be a bean-shooter!"

00:45:30.500 --> 00:45:35.750
"Great guns! We can't get back!
We're prisoners — exactly like
Maple White!"

00:47:15.250 --> 00:47:18.750
"That means our folks
is still alive!"

00:47:25.250 --> 00:47:33.000
"It may mean dose cannibules
what drop dat rock down on us
last night am cookin' 'em!"

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:21.000
"What are you thinking of, Paula
— in this lost world of ours?"

00:48:33.250 --> 00:48:41.750
"I was thinking if my father
were still alive — he would have
seen our fire — and come to us."

00:50:15.750 --> 00:50:21.750
"An Allosaurus — a meat-eater —
the most vicious pest of the
ancient world."

00:52:29.750 --> 00:52:38.250
"Through your careless haste a
wood-tick of an as yet
unclassified species has been
lost to science!"

00:54:17.000 --> 00:54:27.750
"I'm going up the tree to spot a
safer place to camp. In this
moonlight the whole plateau will
be visible."

00:58:12.500 --> 00:58:15.000
"What is it?"

00:58:48.750 --> 00:58:55.786
"Jocko goin' be mighty lonesome
down here wifout Miss Paula — he
climb dat big rock dis mo'nin'
to git to her — he cain't bear

00:58:55.796 --> 00:58:57.250
dat girl outen his sight!"

00:59:08.500 --> 00:59:16.000
"I 'ave a hidea! After supper
bring all those 'ammocks into
the tent!"

00:59:22.750 --> 00:59:34.750
"At daybreak we'll move to the
cave — and leave the trees to
this ape, or man, or missing
link — or whatever it is!"

01:00:46.500 --> 01:00:57.820
"Now that we've found these
caves we could live here the
rest of our lives — if we had
some weapon capable of making a

01:00:57.830 --> 01:00:59.750
dent in a dinosaur!"

01:01:06.250 --> 01:01:10.000
"I am now inventing
such a weapon."

01:01:15.750 --> 01:01:26.773
"I found no signs of your
father, Paula — but Roxton is
exploring a passage that leads
downward — he may have news for

01:01:26.783 --> 01:01:27.250
you."

01:03:12.750 --> 01:03:22.750
"When I release the tree it will
snap back with terrific force —
hurling the rock through the air
—"

01:03:32.000 --> 01:03:36.750
"And by calculating the curve
the rock will describe as it
rushes through the air —"

01:03:39.750 --> 01:03:43.250
"A curve? Nonsense! Any school
boy could tell you the rock will
describe a parabola."

01:03:44.750 --> 01:03:46.250
"A curve!"

01:03:50.750 --> 01:03:53.250
"A parabola!"

01:04:09.750 --> 01:04:14.750
"I was right — you
described a curve!"

01:06:17.250 --> 01:06:22.500
"We all is makin' a rope
ladder to git you all down on!"

01:06:27.250 --> 01:06:34.250
"Fine! But how in thunder do
you expect to get it up to us?"

01:06:37.000 --> 01:06:42.750
"Bring Miss Paula to call Jocko
— he'll climb anything to get to
her!"

01:07:22.000 --> 01:07:32.250
"There's only a small cave above
this — but your father must be
somewhere on the plateau — it's
impossible to get away!"

01:07:35.250 --> 01:07:40.000
"Do you mean we are
to be here — always?"

01:07:44.750 --> 01:07:52.500
"What difference does it make
where we are, Paula — so long as
we are — together?"

01:08:07.250 --> 01:08:11.250
"But your engagement
to — Gladys?"

01:08:17.000 --> 01:08:26.250
"Paula, dear, we're as cut off
from the world and its
obligations and promises as if
we were on the moon."

01:08:45.500 --> 01:08:53.000
"I'm going to ask Professor
Summerlee to marry us. You know
he used to be a minister."

01:09:12.500 --> 01:09:19.000
"Go hunt up the professors — I
have something to — tell Paula
—"

01:10:24.750 --> 01:10:28.250
"What will your fellow
scientists say when I tell of
this in London?"

01:10:30.500 --> 01:10:34.500
"They'll call you just what you
called me in London — a dam'
liar!"

01:13:37.250 --> 01:13:44.000
"That dormant volcano is waking!
The whole plateau will be
covered in lava!"

01:14:18.500 --> 01:14:25.750
"But this has been going on for
hours — I'm afraid they're lost
—"

01:14:27.750 --> 01:14:39.000
"If they get back we must be
here to show them the way to the
tunnel opening. It's their only
hope of escape!"

01:19:16.250 --> 01:19:24.500
"The professors and Mr. Malone
are safe — they'll be here in a
minute or two!"

01:25:58.250 --> 01:26:02.250
"Ah certainly hopes
dat mud holds!"

01:26:17.250 --> 01:26:22.250
"We are back in the world
— and its obligations."

01:26:46.750 --> 01:26:56.750
"You made Gladys promise to wait
for you — you must keep your
part of that promise. This is —
goodbye."

01:28:26.250 --> 01:28:34.500
"Ed thinks a great deal of you,
Paula — and love is too precious
a thing to be cast aside
lightly."

01:28:39.250 --> 01:28:42.250
"My decision is
absolutely final."

01:29:45.500 --> 01:29:53.750
"I'm Major Hibbard, of the
Brazilian Geodetic Survey.
Yesterday I saw a cloud of smoke
over the plateau —"

01:29:58.250 --> 01:30:02.500
"And we smoked out
quite a rat, Major!"

01:30:23.250 --> 01:30:30.250
"I'd give my entire personal
fortune to get that beast to
London — alive!"

01:30:42.250 --> 01:30:55.451
"I'll send for men to dredge out
this stream — build a steel cage
and a raft — and when the big
rains come next month, we might

01:30:55.461 --> 01:30:57.750
float your 'rat' out!"

01:31:00.500 --> 01:31:09.500
"If we could get it on a raft as
far as the Amazon — we could
charter a steamer from there!"

01:32:24.500 --> 01:32:32.500
"Gladys Hungerford is in the
front row. She must be very
proud — of Ed."

01:32:46.250 --> 01:32:53.500
"A year ago when I told you
these monsters are not extinct,
you jeered at me —"

01:32:58.750 --> 01:33:06.000
"Tonight you will not jeer — for
I have brought back a living
proof of my statement!"

01:33:33.750 --> 01:33:41.000
"May I ask what you mean when
you say 'a living proof', my
dear Challenger?"

01:33:45.500 --> 01:33:55.750
"A live Brontosaurus — one
hundred and ten feet long from
the tip of its nose to the end
of its tail."

01:34:09.500 --> 01:34:16.000
"And just where is your
'living proof' now, professor?"

01:34:21.000 --> 01:34:27.911
"Mr. Edward Malone, through
whose efforts this expedition
was financed, is now
superintending the unloading of

01:34:27.921 --> 01:34:30.500
the monster from the ship we
chartered —"

01:34:45.750 --> 01:34:55.244
"The message I have been
expecting has come! I'll inform
you in a moment when and where
the Brontosaurus will be on

01:34:55.254 --> 01:34:55.750
view!"

01:35:09.500 --> 01:35:16.250
"As we swung the cage out over
the dock the cables broke —"

01:35:20.750 --> 01:35:30.000
"The fall smashed the cage — and
it got out! It's running wild —
the streets are in an uproar!"

01:36:00.000 --> 01:36:04.750
"My Brontosaurus has escaped!
Keep off the streets — until I
recapture it!"

01:36:09.250 --> 01:36:13.750
"This is an outrage — we've been
hoaxed again — Challenger should
be run out of town!"

01:40:06.750 --> 01:40:10.500
"How d'ydo, Gladys.
You see I'm — back."

01:40:15.250 --> 01:40:23.000
"You certainly have proved
yourself to be a hero, Ed."

01:40:32.750 --> 01:40:40.500
"And, as you really owe it all
to me, I hope you'll forgive me
— for not waiting."

01:40:41.750 --> 01:40:43.750
"My husband, Percy Potts."

01:40:53.250 --> 01:41:00.000
"May I ask what great stunt,
what heroic deed Mr. Potts
performed — to win you, Gladys?"

01:41:05.500 --> 01:41:12.250
"That was just my girlish whim!
Percy is a clerk at a store, and
has never been out of London in
his life!"

01:41:15.500 --> 01:41:19.250
"Excuse me!"

01:41:30.250 --> 01:41:33.000
"She didn't wait for
me — she's married!"

01:42:01.000 --> 01:42:03.500
"That's Sir John
Roxton — sportsman."
